{
  "title": "Privtrans: Automatically Partitioning Programs for Privilege Separation",
  "authors": [
    "David Brumley",
    "Dawn Song"
  ],
  "year": 2004,
  "arxivId": "",
  "abstract": "Privtrans is a tool that automatically adds privilege separation to programs, allowing them to run with reduced privileges and minimizing the damage of potential security exploits. It works by partitioning a program into two parts: a privileged monitor and an unprivileged slave, which communicate through a secure interface.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Privilege separation is a technique that partitions a program into two parts: a <strong>privileged monitor</strong> and an <strong>unprivileged slave</strong>. The monitor handles all privileged operations, while the slave executes everything else. This approach reduces the attack surface of a program and minimizes the damage of potential security exploits.",
